<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Modeling with Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you have a tank that holds a 100 liters of pure water.  At
some point, you turn on a faucet that causes salt water to flow into
the tank.  Three liters flow in every minute and there are 20 grams
of salt in every liter.  At the same time, you begin to drain water
out of the tank at the rate of 3 liters per minute.</p>
        <p>Using this information, I we can find a differential equation for
<m>S(t)</m>, the number of grams of salt in the tank as a function of time.
%<em>Think in terms of rates!</em></p>
        <p><em>Important:</em>  To set up the equation, think in terms of rates!</p>
        <p>How many grams of salt enters the tank every minute?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's now try to figure out how much salt flows out of the
tank.  Remember that <m>S</m> measures the amount of salt in the tank.</p>
        <p>If there are <m>S</m> grams of salt in the tank, what is the
concentration of salt?  That is, how many grams are in each liter?
(Assume that the water is well mixed.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If three liters of solution are drained out every minute, at what
  rate is salt leaving the tank?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now the rate of change of the amount of salt in the tank is
  the difference of the rate at which salt enters and rate at which
  salt leaves.  This fact may be expressed as a differential equation.
  Write this differential equation below.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch a graph of <m>dS/dt</m> as a function of <m>S</m> and use it to give a
  rough sketch of the slope field.  On the slope field, sketch the the
  solution that corresponds to the initial value <m>S(0) = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>S(t)</m> if there is initially no salt in the tank.
  That is, find the solution of the differential equation for which
  <m>S(0)=0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch your solution below.  What happens to the amount of
  salt in the tank after a very long time?  Does this agree with what
  you found using the slope field?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>When a baby is small, she grows rapidly.  However, as she
  grows, the rate of growth slows down.  A simple model is that the
  rate of change in the baby's weight is <em>inversely</em> proportional
  to the baby's weight.  If we let <m>w(t)</m> be the baby's weight as a
  function of <m>t</m>, write a   simple differential equation that
  expresses this assumption.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Assume that at birth, the baby weighs 8 pounds and one month later,
  she weighs 20 pounds.  That is, <m>w(0)=8</m> and <m>w(1)=20</m>.  Solve the
  differential equation you found above and sketch the solution.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
